## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of user-space vs kernel-space concept interplay, synchronization primitives, and designing a lazy I/O cache with proper wakeups.

## Key Concepts

- Per-block state machine (EMPTY, PENDING, LOADED, ERROR)
- Fast path vs slow path with contention handling
- Wait queues (futex/condvar) and wakeup semantics
- Avoiding thundering herd with CAS and double-checked locking
- Error propagation from I/O to callers

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  void *data;
  atomic_int state; // 0=EMPTY,1=PENDING,2=LOADED,3=ERROR
  int waiters;
  pthread_mutex_t mutex;
  pthread_cond_t cond;
} cache_entry;

#define EMPTY 0
#define PENDING 1
#define LOADED 2
#define ERROR 3

void* block_read(int block_id) {
  cache_entry *entry = &cache[block_id];

  // Fast path
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    return entry->data;
  }

  pthread_mutex_lock(&entry->mutex);

  // Double-check after acquiring lock
  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == LOADED) {
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);
    return entry->data;
  }

  // Transition to PENDING if first thread
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&entry->state, &EMPTY, PENDING)) {
    enqueue_io_request(block_id);
  }

  // Block until I/O completes
  while (atomic_load(&entry->state) == PENDING) {
    entry->waiters++;
    pthread_cond_wait(&entry->cond, &entry->mutex);
    entry->waiters--;
  }

  pthread_mutex_unlock(&entry->mutex);

  if (atomic_load(&entry->state) == ERROR) {
    return NULL; // Propagate error
  }

  return entry->data;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of inter-process sharing via mmap, page cache semantics, and memory visibility without user-space locking.

## Key Concepts
- MAP_SHARED page mappings
- Page cache dirty bits and writeback
- Cache coherence and TLB effects
- Lightweight synchronization in shared memory (CAS, acquire/release)

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo: writer uses CAS to acquire lock, writes, then releases
uint32_t *lock = (uint32_t*)addr; // shared region
while (__sync_lock_test_and_set(lock, 1)) {}
// write 8 bytes to region after lock
memcpy((void*)(addr+8), data, 8);
__sync_lock_release(lock);

// Reader checks lock before reading
while (*lock) {} // wait for writer
memcpy(data, (void*)(addr+8), 8);
```

## Tradeoffs
Lock: Simple, ensures exclusive access but blocks readers during writes.
Seqlock: Readers can proceed concurrently but must retry if write occurs mid-read; better for read-heavy workloads.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of memory-management fundamentals in operating systems, including COW semantics, page-table updates, and race-free fault handling.

## Key Concepts

- Copy-On-Write semantics and reference counting
- Page-table entries and flags (COW, writable)
- Race conditions during concurrent faults on the same page
- Minimal fault handler design and synchronization primitives

## Code Example

```c
// Per-page lock structure
typedef struct {
    spinlock_t lock;
    int ref_count;
} COWPage;

// Global table mapping physical frames to COW metadata
COWPage *cow_table[MAX_FRAMES];

void handle_write_fault(Process *p, VirtAddr va) {
    PageTableEntry *pte = walk_page_table(p->pgd, va);

    if (!pte || !(pte->flags & COW_BIT))
        return; // Not a COW page fault

    PhysAddr frame = pte->frame;
    COWPage *cow = cow_table[frame];

    spin_lock(&cow->lock);

    // Double-check after acquiring lock
    if (cow->ref_count > 1) {
        // Allocate new frame
        PhysAddr new_frame = allocate_frame();

        // Copy content
        memcpy(phys_to_virt(new_frame), phys_to_virt(frame), PAGE_SIZE);

        // Decrement original reference count
        cow->ref_count--;

        // Update current process page table
        pte->frame = new_frame;
        pte->flags = (pte->flags & ~COW_BIT) | WRITABLE_BIT;

        // Add new frame to COW table with ref_count = 1
        cow_table[new_frame] = cow;
        cow_table[new_frame]->ref_count = 1;
    } else {
        // Last reference - just make writable
        pte->flags = (pte->flags & ~COW_BIT) | WRITABLE_BIT;
    }

    spin_unlock(&cow->lock);
}
```

## Race Handling

Using per-page locks ensures that only one process can handle the fault for a given physical page at a time. The double-check pattern (checking ref_count before and after acquiring the lock) prevents redundant duplications. CAS (Compare-And-Swap) can also be used to atomically decrement the reference count and allocate a new page in a single operation.

